WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.020
Michael Hauge: Desire just means there is
something that the character wants.

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:08.300
It isn’t necessarily confined to sexual
or romantic desire.

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:13.219
That just happens to be one of those movies
there was just this elicit sexual…I don’t

00:00:13.219 --> 00:00:20.130
know if it was really elicit in 9 and 1/2
weeks or how you’d categorize it…on a

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:23.820
visible level the desire could be to win a
competition.

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:32.000
In fact if you look at Hollywood movies probably
90 percent of Hollywood movies are about characters

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:35.160
who want to one of basically three or four
things.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:41.970
That is, they either want to win a competition
or win the love of another character.

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:46.890
So that encompasses all sports movies and
all romantic comedies and love stories or

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:53.190
romantic hyphenate movies or they want to
stop something bad from happening.

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:59.500
That is all horror movies, most science fiction
movies, all thrillers, all murder mysteries.

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:04.059
Although Hollywood really doesn’t do murder-mysteries
much, but that’s about stopping a killer

00:01:04.059 --> 00:01:06.200
from getting away with the murder.

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:14.740
They want to escape from a bad situation,
like GREAT ESCAPE or PANIC ROOM or HUNT FOR

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:19.750
THE WILDERPEOPLE or they want to retrieve
something.

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:24.510
They want to get something of value and own
it, possess it at the end of the story.

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:30.150
So that is all heist movies, it’s kidnapping
stories rescue stories, we’ve got to go

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:32.720
get this person and get them to safety.

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:39.480
And if you look at the top box office movies
for any top given year, I bet probably 90

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:42.970
out of a 100 are going to fall into one of
those four categories of goal.

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:46.900
Film Courage: So desire doesn’t necessary
mean romantic interest.

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:53.479
It can mean conquering…in the case of the
Helen Mirren movie you mentioned earlier [EYE

00:01:53.479 --> 00:01:57.970
IN THE SKY] wanting to find the bad guy, that
conquer is the desire that moves it through,

00:01:57.970 --> 00:02:03.000
but conflict is trying to find these bad guys
who have moved throughout the area.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:04.000
Michael Hauge: Yeah.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:05.000
That is part of the conflict.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:06.409
Her goal…that’s a stop movie.

00:02:06.409 --> 00:02:11.940
She wants to stop the bad guys from leaving
the house…because it is discovered that

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:15.922
they are bad guys or they are arming some
bad guy.

00:02:15.922 --> 00:02:21.540
So they are going to go into some populace
place and hurt a lot of people and she wants

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:22.970
to stop them.

00:02:22.970 --> 00:02:25.890
Part of the conflict is they are going to
find them, they have to make sure it’s them,

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:28.120
they’ve got to get the drone in position.

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:37.090
But part of the conflict is what happens when
an innocent bystander comes into the place

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:42.550
where the collateral damage is going to be
done, if there is an innocent person there,

00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:49.810
it starts a moral dilemma of is one person’s
life worth risking lives of one hundred to

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:50.860
save?

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:54.460
So then there becomes this moral conflict,
too.

00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:57.772
They are all obstacles to her doing what she
wants, which is to stop the bad guys.

00:02:57.772 --> 00:02:58.272
Question for the Viewers: Which of these categories
does your movie fall into?

